\id XXA
\h Chapters and Verses
\toc1 Books, Chapters, and Verses in the King James Bible
\toc2 Chapters and Verses
\toc3 Numbering
\mt1 Books, Chapters, and Verses in the King James Bible
\ip These tables are for a reader who looks up a verse of this Bible in the King James Bible, or a verse of the King James Bible here. They are mostly of the Old Testament; in the New Testament the two number alike, except for the few verses that the Byzantine text lacks or places elsewhere, which the tables of Matthew, Luke, Acts and Romans give.\f + \ft The tables of chapters and verses were worked out from the words of the two translations and from STEP Bible’s table of the ways in which Bibles number their verses (data created by www.STEPBible.org based on work at Tyndale House Cambridge, CC BY 4.0).\f* This Old Testament is Brenton’s translation of the Greek; the King James Bible’s is translated from the Hebrew. The two name most books alike, and number most chapters and verses alike. Where they don’t, the tables give this edition’s name or number on the left and the King James Bible’s on the right. Where one Bible lacks what the other has, the word \it missing\it* stands on its side.
\ip The numbers differ for several reasons. The Greek arranges some passages in another order than the Hebrew, as the prophecies of Jeremias against the nations. Each has passages that the other lacks. Some chapters begin a verse or more earlier or later. And one often divides a verse where the other doesn’t, so that the verses after it are one higher or lower until the two agree again.
\is1 Books
\ip The King James Bible names these books otherwise, or sets them apart in its Apocrypha, or lacks them. Some are parts of books here. Susanna stands before the first chapter of Daniel, and Bel and the Dragon after the last; the Song of the Three Children runs from {DAG 3:24} to {bare DAG 3:90}. Psalm 151 ends the Psalms. The additions to Esther are its verses with letters, except {bare ESG 1:1s}, and also {ESG 1:1}, {bare ESG 5:1} and {bare ESG 5:2}. This edition’s 2 Esdras is the King James Bible’s Ezra, not the 2 Esdras of its Apocrypha, which this edition lacks and which comes last. A book that the table doesn’t name has the same name in both.
{names}
\is1 Chapters and Verses
\ip Each table is headed by the book’s name in each Bible. A number alone is a whole chapter or psalm, with its verses numbered alike in both. Otherwise the chapter stands before the colon and the verses after it. Where a run of verses or psalms stands beside another, as {bare GEN 32:2-33} beside {kjv bare GEN 32:2-33}, they answer one to one in order. One verse may stand beside two or more, or two beside one. The rows follow the order of this edition, so a King James verse may have to be sought down the right-hand side.
\ip A chapter or a verse that the tables don’t name has the same number in both, except for these. A verse that Brenton numbers with a letter, as {PRO 22:8a}, is usually an addition of the Greek, and isn’t in the King James Bible unless the tables give its place there. The books and parts of books that the King James Bible sets apart in its Apocrypha, which are named above, aren’t compared verse by verse. Nor are the titles of the psalms, which are explained over the table of the Psalms.
{books}
\ip Brenton numbers the psalms as the Greek does. The Greek joins some psalms that the Hebrew divides, and divides others, so that most psalms are one lower here. Brenton also often counts a psalm’s title as its first verse, or its first two. The King James Bible doesn’t number the titles, so these verses have no place on the right.
{psalms}
